    page = f"""<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-theme="dark"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="A first-principles investigation of how generation-time text watermarking works, from a weighted coin to Gemma."><title>How does a text watermark work?</title><style>{css}</style><noscript><style>.noscript{{display:block}}.viz,.opening-viz{{display:none}}</style></noscript></head><body><div class="topbar"><div class="shell"><span class="brand">Text watermarking / an open-model investigation</span><button id="theme">Use light page</button><span class="progress" id="progress"></span></div></div><header class="hero"><div class="shell"><h1>How does a text watermark work?</h1><p class="dek">Images can hide tiny changes in millions of pixels. Plain text has nowhere obvious to put them. I built a small watermark from the ground up to find where the signal lives, how a checker sees it, and how easily ordinary edits disturb it.</p><div class="premise-strip"><div><b>The puzzle</b>Copy and paste keeps the words and drops the file wrapper.</div><div><b>The mechanism</b>A key leans on many acceptable token choices during generation.</div><div><b>The test</b>A checker looks for the accumulated preference in copied text.</div></div></div></header><main class="article shell"><aside class="noscript readout"><b>Scripts are off.</b> The complete article, measurements, tables, citations, code, and conclusions remain below. The scripted figures are hidden.</aside>{article}</main><footer class="footer"><div class="shell">Built from committed Stage 1 through Stage 8 evidence. The page uses no external scripts, fonts, storage, model calls, or runtime network requests.</div></footer><script id="evidence" type="application/json">{compact(payload)}</script><script>{script}</script></body></html>"""
